Inter-Process Communication (IPC)
Signals and Remote Procedure Calls (RPC)

What is Inter-Process Communication?
Inter-Process Communication (IPC) refers to the mechanisms provided by an operating system that allow two or more processes to communicate and coordinate with each other.
Since processes generally have separate memory spaces, one process cannot simply access another process's private memory. IPC provides controlled mechanisms for processes to exchange information, synchronize activities, and notify each other about events.
Main Purposes of IPC
IPC is commonly used for:
•	Data exchange
•	Process synchronization
•	Event notification
•	Resource sharing
•	Client-server communication
•	Process coordination
•	Communication in distributed systems
Common IPC Techniques
Some commonly studied IPC mechanisms include:
•	Pipes
•	Message Queues
•	Shared Memory
•	Semaphores
•	Sockets
•	Signals
•	Remote Procedure Calls (RPC)
•	Memory-Mapped Communication
This project focuses on Signals and Remote Procedure Calls (RPC).
4. Signals
What is a Signal?
A signal is a notification sent to a process or thread to inform it that a particular event has occurred. Signals are primarily used for event notification and process control rather than transferring large amounts of data.
Linux provides several types of signals with predefined meanings.
Examples of Signals
Signal	Typical Purpose
SIGINT	Interrupt from the terminal, commonly generated using Ctrl+C
SIGTERM	Request for process termination
SIGKILL	Forcefully terminates a process
SIGSTOP	Stops a process
SIGCONT	Continues a stopped process
SIGCHLD	Indicates that a child process has changed state
SIGALRM	Indicates that a timer/alarm has expired
SIGSEGV	Indicates an invalid memory-access fault

Linux also provides POSIX real-time signals, which offer additional functionality such as queued signals and associated data.
How Signals Work
The basic process is illustrated below:
+-------------+
|  Process A  |
+------+------+
       |
       | Send Signal
       v
+-------------------+
| Operating System  |
+---------+---------+
          |
          | Deliver Signal
          v
+-------------+
|  Process B  |
+------+------+
       |
       v
+----------------+
| Signal Handler |
+----------------+



Applications of Signals
Signals can be used for:
•	Process termination
•	Process interruption
•	Timer notifications
•	Child-process notifications
•	Configuration reload notifications
•	Handling exceptional events
5. Remote Procedure Call (RPC)
What is RPC?
Remote Procedure Call (RPC) is an IPC mechanism that allows a program to request a procedure or function to execute in another process. The other process may be running:
•	On the same computer
•	On another computer in a network
•	On a remote system
RPC is commonly used in client-server and distributed applications.
Basic Idea of RPC
A normal function call might look like:
result = calculate(10, 20);

With RPC, the requested procedure may execute in another process.
+----------------+
| Client Process |
+-------+--------+
        |
        | RPC Request
        v
+----------------+
| Server Process |
+-------+--------+
        |
        | Execute Procedure
        v
+----------------+
|     Result     |
+-------+--------+
        |
        | RPC Response
        v
+----------------+
| Client Process |
+----------------+

The RPC system handles many of the communication details involved in sending the request and receiving the result.
